TALE Tale

ABOUT
  Tale helps people and AI agents communicate project goals, expectations, boundaries, and evidence in a clear, reusable way.

GOAL
  Create a visual Tale editor delivered as both a cross-platform Electron app and a Chrome extension. The diagram should help people organise human-language notes and relationships before sharing them with an AI agent.
  Purpose defines Product.

PRODUCT
  People can use the same Tale editor as an Electron desktop app or a Chrome extension. Both builds open and save JSON projects and compile identical deterministic .tale output. Tags and Skills are reusable notes with one editable text value each.
  Keep the diagram easy to navigate with precise dragging, arrows, mouse and touchpad zoom, and clear icons. Selecting a note should show its title and one human-language text box.
  Product guides Architecture.
  Product includes Deployment.

ARCHITECTURE
  Use TypeScript. Share the model, compiler, SVG editor, templates, deployment content, and UI between Electron and Chrome. Keep Electron and Chrome filesystem, lifecycle, and permission code behind separate platform adapters. Do not duplicate shared behaviour. Pass only validated data across platform boundaries.
  Architecture constrains Dependencies.

DEPLOYMENT
  Deploy only the generated .tale file to the selected project’s .tale directory. Keep the editor JSON outside deployment. Update the selected agent instructions without removing unrelated content..
  Deployment must satisfy Deploy exact Tale bytes.
  Deployment must satisfy Keep project JSON out of deployment.
  Deployment must satisfy Preserve the editor project.

DEPENDENCIES
  Use the existing Electron, TypeScript, and Biome tools. Do not add UI or SVG libraries. Ask before adding any other dependency.

AGREEMENT
  Discuss unclear requirements and agree on scope and completion checks before editing. Respect approval already given.
  Agreement sets limits on Changes.
  Agreement defines Allowed changes.
  Agreement defines Forbidden changes.
  Agreement defines Approval required.

CHANGES
  Make the smallest change that solves the agreed problem. Deny unrelated changes. Ask before refactoring, renaming, moving, or deleting unrelated code.

SCOPE Allowed changes
  Change only the files needed for the agreed task.

SCOPE Forbidden changes
  Do not change .git or unrelated project files.

SCOPE Approval required
  Ask before changing Tale rules, agent instructions, the README, package or lock files, TypeScript configuration, or Biome configuration.

QUALITY
  Keep TypeScript strict. Run Biome lint and formatting checks, and keep the application code readable.
  Quality requires Testing.

TESTING
  Every TypeScript source file has a sibling test file. Test observable behaviour and run the relevant unit, Electron interaction, and Chrome package checks.
  Testing provides evidence for Proof.

DETERMINISM Deterministic output
  The same saved JSON project must generate exactly the same .tale bytes after every build. Diagram layout and colours must not change the output.
  Deterministic output must be demonstrated in Proof.

AGENTIC_TESTS
  An agent may add focused tests for its task. Do not change a passing test merely to approve a new implementation. Preserve tests once their agreed proof passes.
  Agentic tests adds checks to Testing.

PROOF
  Run type checking, lint, formatting, unit tests, Electron end-to-end tests, and Chrome extension package checks. Verify that Electron and Chrome compile identical Tale bytes. Review the final diff and stop when the agreed checks pass.

REQUIREMENT Deploy exact Tale bytes
  Deploy the exact Tale bytes generated from the saved project JSON. Check the installed file against the compiler output.
  Deploy exact Tale bytes is verified by Exercise actual deployment.

REQUIREMENT Keep project JSON out of deployment
  Keep the editor project JSON out of the destination .tale directory. Deploy only the compiled Tale.
  Keep project JSON out of deployment is verified by Exercise actual deployment.

REQUIREMENT Preserve the editor project
  Deployment must leave the source project JSON unchanged.
  Preserve the editor project is verified by Exercise actual deployment.

CHECK Exercise actual deployment
  Exercise Electron and Chrome deployment. Confirm that the installed Tale matches the shared compiler output, no project JSON is installed, the source JSON is preserved, and both builds produce identical Tale bytes.
